<Project Sdk="Aspire.AppHost.Sdk/13.6.0">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <AspireUseCliBundle>true</AspireUseCliBundle>

    <!-- THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO $(AspireCliInvocationMode) HERE, AND IT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT.
         The default — `Path` — is what builds on a clean machine. This project briefly set `Dnx`,
         which resolves the CLI through the unversioned `Aspire.Cli` package and so honours a
         .config/dotnet-tools.json pin, giving the repository control of the DCP and dashboard
         version rather than taking whatever the machine has installed. That is a genuinely better
         property and it does not survive contact with CI. Both halves of it broke, each in a way
         that names nothing useful:

           * `Dnx` makes the CLI bundle mandatory and its absence fatal. On a runner with no
             `aspire` on PATH the build falls through to an automatic `dnx` bootstrap of the
             `aspire.cli setup` command — a ~182 MB download — which exited 1 with its own output
             swallowed, surfacing only as error ASPIRE009 from inside
             Aspire.Hosting.AppHost.targets, raised against this csproj after every other project
             in the solution had already compiled.
           * The .config/dotnet-tools.json entry that `Dnx` needs breaks `dotnet tool restore`
             itself on linux-x64. With `aspire.cli` in the manifest, every OTHER tool in it fails
             to resolve — "the commands contained in the package are aspire" for reportgenerator,
             apicompat, sbom-tool and nbgv alike — and the restore partially fails. Removing the
             entry restores all four. Reproduced on a cleared cache, so it is the manifest and not
             stale state; it does not reproduce on macos-arm64, which is why a green local build
             said nothing about it.

         Both were diagnosed in the sibling indicia-focus-chat repository, which was generated from
         the same template and failed identically; this change is that one propagated. The cost of
         the default is that DCP and the dashboard follow the installed CLI rather than a number
         here, leaving the SDK version in the Project element above as the only place carrying
         13.6.0. `make check-aspire` is what asserts the CLI is present at all. Re-add `Dnx` only
         alongside a CI run that proves both problems above are gone. -->

    <UserSecretsId>2dceb9a7-95b2-44fd-8e73-40400e8895a5</UserSecretsId>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Aspire.Hosting.Keycloak" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
